<Docs title="Installation — Matra docs" description="Which packages exist, which are MIT, and which need a subscription." section="Getting started" heading="Installation">
  <p>The core is all most projects need.</p>
  <pre><code>npm i @matrajs/core</code></pre>

  <h2>Framework bindings</h2>
  <pre><code>npm i @matrajs/react   # useEditor, useEditorState, EditorContent
npm i @matrajs/vue     # useEditor, EditorContent</code></pre>
  <p>
    Both are MIT and both are thin — the editor does not care which framework renders around it,
    and neither binding re-implements anything.
  </p>

  <h2>The paid packages</h2>
  <pre><code>npm i @matrajs/ai      # streaming edits
npm i @matrajs/collab  # authority, rebasing, remote cursors</code></pre>
  <p>
    These are source-available under a commercial licence rather than MIT, and come from a
    private registry. Free for evaluation, development, testing, teaching, personal projects and
    teams of fewer than three developers — see <a href="/licence">the licence</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    Nothing checks a licence at runtime and nothing phones home. A subscription is what lets
    <code>npm install</code> reach these packages, not what lets your app run.
  </div>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Any bundler. The packages ship ESM and CJS with types.</li>
    <li>No peer dependencies for the core.</li>
    <li>Node 18+ for the parts that run on a server — Markdown needs no DOM.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
